[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering the Vue.js Reactivity System: ref, reactive, toRef, and toRefs"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Vue.js Composition API provides a powerful and flexible reactivity system. Understanding how to manage state using ref, reactive, toRef, and toRefs is essential for building scalable applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding ref"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The ref function takes an inner value and returns a reactive and mutable ref object. This object has a single property, .value, which points to the internal value. It is typically used for primitive types like strings, numbers, and booleans."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"import { ref } from 'vue';

const count = ref(0);
console.log(count.value); // 0

count.value++;
console.log(count.value); // 1"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Understanding reactive"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike ref, which wraps primitives in an object, the reactive function makes an entire object reactive. The returned object is a Proxy of the original, deeply reactive across nested properties."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"import { reactive } from 'vue';

const state = reactive({
  name: 'Vue Dev',
  version: 3
});

state.version++;"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. The Danger of Destructuring (toRef and toRefs)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"When destructuring properties from a reactive object, you lose reactivity because JavaScript evaluates the values at the time of assignment. Vue provides toRef and toRefs to solve this problem by creating refs that maintain a reactive connection to the source property."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"import { reactive, toRefs } from 'vue';

const user = reactive({
  firstName: 'John',
  lastName: 'Doe'
});

// toRefs converts all properties into individual refs
const { firstName, lastName } = toRefs(user);

// Single property can be converted using toRef
const singleRef = toRef(user, 'firstName');"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices Summary"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use ref for primitive values (strings, numbers, booleans) and standalone variables."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use reactive when managing complex states where properties logically belong together inside an object."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use toRefs when returning properties from setup() to the template while preserving reactivity during destructuring."}]
